{
	"version": 5,
	"id": "BY6J5FynPyHV41_-P",
	"notes": "An Aspect Warrior: a Guardian who has walked into an Aspect Shrine and given themselves over to one narrow art of war for years or decades, matching a Space Marine's own precision on the strength of centuries of focus rather than gene-engineering. Point math (see Xenos/Aeldari/Traits/ Aeldari Physiology.adq): Aeldari Physiology + Keen Battle-Sense + Aspect Training (traits, 249 points) + skills (46 points) = 295 points. Stats: ST 11, DX 16, IQ 12, HT 11, HP 11, Will 13, Per 13, Speed 7.0, Move 12 (sprint x1.5 with Enhanced Move 1/2), Dodge 11 (7 + 3 + Enhanced Dodge 1) — landing exactly on framework.md's Aspect Warrior anchor line, matching DX 16 to a Battle-Brother's own DX 16 exactly as the anchor table intends. This sits in the top half of framework.md's \"Elite human\" tier (200-350) and the bottom of \"Senior agents\" (350-600): a formidable specialist well above a Guardian, but still short of a full Astartes package's own 550-800 total, which the framework reserves for the Exarch a Warrior-Path veteran eventually becomes.\nEnable exactly one Aspect lens below (from Xenos/Aeldari/Traits/Aeldari Physiology.adq, all disabled by default) for the specific shrine this character belongs to; they are small, low-point flavour packages layered on top of this shared Aspect Warrior chassis, not full separate templates, matching this project's Klan and regimental lens precedent. This template also includes \"Path of the Warrior\", enabled, since every Aspect Warrior is, by definition, walking it; disable it and enable a different Path lens instead only for an unusual case (e.g. building a former Aspect Warrior who has since moved on).\n",
	"traits": [
		{
			"id": "TTBkAcEMjXFSRurBs",
			"name": "Aeldari Physiology",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The Aeldari: an ancient, dying, near-immortal species, slender and swift, all born with a latent thread into the warp and all hunted, in the end, by the god their own species' fall gave birth to. This trait is the baseline shared by every Craftworld Aeldari; Path lenses and Aspect lenses below layer specialization on top of it, exactly as the Astartes gene-seed file's Chapter lenses and the Ork Physiology file's Klan lenses do for their own species.\n\nDesign: 140 (Attribute Modifiers) + 10 (Enhanced Move 1/2) + 8 (Acute Senses) + 4 (Aeldari Grace) + 0 (Slender Frame) + 12 (Extended Lifespan 6) + 10 (Psychic Sensitivity) + 0 (Spirit Stone) - 10 (Emotional Intensity) - 5 (Arrogance) - 15 (Slaanesh's Hunger) = 154 points of physiology alone, before Path, Aspect, or skills — landing ST 10, DX 14, IQ 12, HT 10, HP 10, Will 12, Per 12 (Per 13 once Keen Battle-Sense is added by a warrior-path template), Basic Speed 6.25 and Basic Move 9 (Move 6 plus 3, with Enhanced Move 1/2 for sprinting). See the Guardian template for the first full character built on this package.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Aeldari"
			],
			"container_type": "ancestry",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tnD5eTYePq_8Qb1wB",
					"name": "Attribute Modifiers",
					"local_notes": "Design: Framework target (Guardian anchor line, the ancestry baseline every other Aeldari template builds on): ST 10, DX 14, IQ 12, HT 10, HP 10. Increased DX +4 (B15, 20/level, 80) for DX 14 — \"faster and more precise than any baseline human,\" matching the anchor table's own figure without touching ST or HT, which stay human-baseline: Aeldari are quick and precise, not physically overwhelming. Increased IQ +2 (B16, 20/level, 40) for IQ 12 — a subtler, more perceptive mind than a human's on average, per Codex background's consistent \"the Aeldari are wiser and more perceptive than short-lived Humanity\" framing. Increased Basic Speed +0.25 (B17, 5 points per 0.25 level, 5) for reflexes a shade faster even than DX 14 alone implies — the same design choice this project's Astartes Attribute Modifiers makes for \"faster reflexes than baseline humans\" without inflating every DX skill. Basic Move +3 (B17, 5/level, 15) at the user's direction: the Aeldari are the fastest-moving people in the galaxy, and even a Guardian outpaces a human (Move 9). 80+40+5+15 = 140. Will and Per are left untouched here (both track IQ 12 with no separate bonus): the Guardian anchor's own Per 13 and Aspect Warrior's Will 13 come from Keen Battle-Sense and Aspect Training below, not from this universal package, because a Farseer's own anchor (Per 15 exactly equal to IQ 15) shows the extra Per point is a warrior's battlefield habit, not a species-wide trait — baking it in here would overshoot the Farseer line once her own IQ is bought up.",
					"tags": [
						"Physical"
					],
					"base_points": 140,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "dx",
							"amount": 4
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "iq",
							"amount": 2
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "basic_speed",
							"amount": 0.25
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "basic_move",
							"amount": 3
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tvZponUiVNzHd97sK",
					"name": "Enhanced Move (Ground)",
					"reference": "B52",
					"local_notes": "Top sprinting speed x1.5. Combat Move unchanged.\n\nLore: Aeldari run with a lightness and speed no human can match; in the fiction they cross open ground before an enemy can bring guns to bear.\n\nDesign: Enhanced Move (Ground) 1/2 (B52, half level 10) at the user's direction, matching the Astartes half level.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Aeldari"
					],
					"points_per_level": 20,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 0.5
				},
				{
					"id": "Tii05VGhOwFXKCcdT",
					"name": "Acute Senses",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Aeldari sight and hearing are famously sharper than a human's — able to read the wind off a craftworld's wraithbone spires, or track a single voice through a warhost's roar. Consistently depicted across Codex background and the Path trilogy (a Ranger's eyesight in particular is played as near-supernatural).\n\nDesign: Acute Vision 2 (B35, 2/level, 4) plus Acute Hearing 2 (B35, 2/level, 4) = 8 points, the same math this project's Astartes Occulobe/Lyman's Ear entries use for the \"raw acuity\" half of their build, without the Protected Sense half (Aeldari have no fused eye/ear armour organ to shrug off flash and concussion the way an Astartes implant does).",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Aeldari"
					],
					"container_type": "meta_trait",
					"children": [
						{
							"id": "tZkKKu6uCT5yozDE8",
							"name": "Acute Vision",
							"reference": "B35",
							"points_per_level": 2,
							"can_level": true,
							"levels": 2
						},
						{
							"id": "td9Yy3hRCONYX5R-p",
							"name": "Acute Hearing",
							"reference": "B35",
							"points_per_level": 2,
							"can_level": true,
							"levels": 2
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "ti3g3xiCRytH2KrOm",
					"name": "Aeldari Grace",
					"reference": "B21",
					"local_notes": "Appearance (Attractive).\n\nLore: Aeldari are consistently described, in the fiction and in their own art, as slender, fine-boned and unnervingly graceful even to other Aeldari — a species that finds its own dying beauty worth mourning.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Appearance, Attractive tier (B21), 4 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Aeldari"
					],
					"base_points": 4
				},
				{
					"id": "tk4gvvTlXhRzBHxF-",
					"name": "Slender Frame",
					"local_notes": "Lore: An Aeldari body is light and fine-boned next to a human's, let alone an Ork's or an Astartes': speed and precision over mass. Codex background and the Path trilogy both play Aeldari as easy to underestimate physically and easy to actually hurt once a fight turns physical.\n\nDesign: 0-point lore flag: the mechanical consequence is what's absent, not present — this package buys no Extra Hit Points and no Damage Resistance the way the Astartes and Ork physiology files do, so Aeldari HP tracks bare ST with no bonus. This entry documents why, rather than pricing anything itself.",
					"tags": [
						"Xenos",
						"Aeldari"
					],
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "tEsOM-4K2AiwKOZBG",
					"name": "Extended Lifespan",
					"reference": "B53",
					"local_notes": "x64 natural lifespan (roughly 5,000 years before natural death; each level doubles it).\n\nLore: Aeldari live for millennia baseline, and the oldest craftworld seers and named heroes (Eldrad Ulthran chief among them) are shown outlasting even that by a wide margin — treated here as an exceptional, GM-adjudicated extension on top of this baseline rather than a fixed higher purchase every Aeldari makes.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Extended Lifespan (B53, 2 points/level, each level doubling natural lifespan) at level 6: x64 (about 5,000 years for an 80-year baseline), 12 points. Level 6 gives the \"millennia\" the lore asks for; levels 1-2 would only reach x2-x4 (a few centuries). No base cost: the whole price is the levels.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Aeldari"
					],
					"points_per_level": 2,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 6
				},
				{
					"id": "tWQ-M4oe8lfHyT-ra",
					"name": "Aeldari Psychic Sensitivity",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Every Aeldari is born with a latent thread into the warp — a legacy of Isha and Cegorach's blessing, or of the psychic shock of the Fall, depending which telling you credit — where among humans the gift is a vanishingly rare mutation. Most Aeldari never develop it past a constant, low background hum of unease near the warp; Warlocks and Farseers are the ones who walk toward it instead of away.\n\nDesign: This project's Aeldari analogue of Imperium/Psykers and the Warp.adq's Unusual Background (Latent Psychic Potential): identical cost (10 points, B261) and identical function (it is the prerequisite for Aeldari Psyker (Talent), Aeldari Warp Empowerment, and every Warp Sorcery-tagged Rune below), but built as its own entry rather than reused verbatim because the Imperium entry's own lore text is explicitly a rare human mutation — false for a species where the gift is universal and latent. Bought once as part of the Aeldari Physiology package below, unlike the Imperium version, which is an optional purchase most humans never make.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Aeldari",
						"Psyker"
					],
					"base_points": 10
				},
				{
					"id": "tZjZzUPU_n3jzocXp",
					"name": "Spirit Stone",
					"local_notes": "Worn on the breast from childhood. At its bearer's death the stone itself catches the escaping soul (automatically, so long as it is worn and intact); a Warlock, Farseer or Spiritseer later retrieves it and carries it to a craftworld's Infinity Circuit. A stone lost, shattered or left behind leaves the soul to Slaanesh (see Slaanesh's Hunger, below). It carries no combat bonus.\n\nLore: Every Craftworld Aeldari wears a spirit stone: a small, wraithbone- cored gem that anchors the soul against the warp and, at death, catches it before Slaanesh does, from which it may later be interred in a craftworld's Infinity Circuit to live on, disembodied, among its ancestors. Consistent, foundational Aeldari background across every edition of Codex: Craftworlds and the Path trilogy.\n\nDesign: A 0-point flag recording that the character wears one (the item is Spirit Stone (Waystone) in wargear/armour.yaml, $300). The stone's real narrative weight, catching a soul at death, is a GM-adjudicated death-and-afterlife outcome and not a combat-turn mechanic, so it is not priced and grants no conditional bonus.",
					"tags": [
						"Xenos",
						"Aeldari",
						"Psyker"
					],
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "tvmA3dgy9fFThbwlm",
					"name": "Emotional Intensity",
					"local_notes": "As Bad Temper (B124), but the triggering emotion can be grief, joy, desire or artistic rapture as easily as anger — whatever the moment's strongest feeling is, not anger specifically. On a failed self-control roll the Aeldari acts on the emotion, not on reason, for the rest of the scene.\n\nLore: Aeldari feel everything more sharply than a human does — joy, grief, desire, fury — and the fiction is explicit that this, not any weakness of will, is why the Path exists at all: without the discipline of devoting centuries to one narrow art at a time, an Aeldari mind burns out or turns inward on itself. Path of the Warrior and Path of the Seer both open on exactly this problem.\n\nDesign: Built as Bad Temper (B124, self-control 12, -10 points) with a broadened trigger, the same \"as Bloodlust, but...\" reflavoring this project's Chapter Gene-Seed: Blood Angels entry uses for Red Thirst: same cost and self-control math, different triggering emotion.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Aeldari"
					],
					"base_points": -10,
					"cr": 12
				},
				{
					"id": "tIMNUXMFUHcKjUs_F",
					"name": "Arrogance Towards Younger Races",
					"reference": "B22",
					"local_notes": "Odious Personal Habit: casual condescension toward humans, Orks, and other 'younger' races.\n\nLore: An Aeldari watching over the ruins of a civilization older than humanity's entire recorded history struggles not to be condescending about it — Codex background and the Path trilogy alike play this as a near-universal Aeldari failing, not a villain trait: even sympathetic Aeldari protagonists talk down to \"mon-keigh\" without quite noticing they're doing it.\n\nDesign: Odious Personal Habit (B22), minor tier, -5 points: a real social cost (reaction penalties from anyone who notices) but not the deliberate cruelty a full Intolerance or Bully disadvantage would represent.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Aeldari"
					],
					"base_points": -5
				},
				{
					"id": "tokgFfJbe7m07N1pU",
					"name": "Slaanesh's Hunger",
					"reference": "B161",
					"local_notes": "As Weirdness Magnet, but the \"weirdness\" is specifically Chaos and warp phenomena drawn to an Aeldari soul's exceptional flavour: Chaos cultists, daemons (Slaaneshi ones above all), and warp-touched omens/hauntings single an Aeldari out over other bystanders, at the GM's discretion, roughly as often as Weirdness Magnet's own frequency guidance suggests.\nThis is also the mechanical flag for the larger, unpriced stake behind it: an Aeldari who dies with no spirit stone drawing their soul clear (see Spirit Stone, above) is devoured by Slaanesh, GM's call on the exact fate — endless torment, absorption into the Dark Prince's being, or worse. Like this project's Black Rage and Wulfen Curse entries, this fate is a narrative endpoint the GM adjudicates when it's dramatically earned, not a disadvantage bought and rolled against at chargen. A functioning Spirit Stone worn at time of death, and recovered promptly, avoids it outright.\n\nLore: Slaanesh, the youngest Chaos god, was born screaming from the psychic shockwave of the Aeldari's own Fall, and has hungered for Aeldari souls above all others' ever since — this is one of the single most consistent, foundational beats of Aeldari background across every edition of Codex: Craftworlds and Codex: Drukhari, and the entire reason spirit stones and the Infinity Circuit exist.\n\nDesign: Weirdness Magnet (B161, -15 points) reused for its \"supernatural trouble finds you more often\" mechanic, standing in for the fact that an Aeldari's soul draws Chaos's attention the way a bright light draws moths. The GM-adjudicated death fate it flags is deliberately not separately priced, per the framework's own guidance to build this honestly rather than as a combat-relevant stat; the Spirit Stone above is what actually buys it off.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Aeldari"
					],
					"base_points": -15
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "tNY6P9SVGfSY4w2TA",
			"name": "Keen Battle-Sense",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Guardian's militia drills, an Aspect Warrior's shrine training, and an Exarch's centuries in the same battle-role all sharpen the same thing: a heightened, ever-present awareness of the battlefield around them. A Farseer's gift runs a different direction entirely (inward and forward, not outward), which is why this is a warrior's-path trait, not part of the universal Aeldari package.\n\nDesign: Increased Perception +1 (B16, 5 points/level): the Guardian anchor's Per 13 over IQ 12, and the Aspect Warrior/Exarch anchors' matching one-point gap over their own IQ, all come from this single trait rather than a bonus baked into Attribute Modifiers, so it can be left out of the Farseer line (whose own anchor is Per == IQ exactly) without distorting that template's math.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Aeldari"
			],
			"base_points": 5,
			"features": [
				{
					"type": "attribute_bonus",
					"attribute": "per",
					"amount": 1
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "T2CfTWxUNrfHgXRXv",
			"name": "Aspect Training",
			"local_notes": "Lore: What a Shrine actually does to a Guardian who commits to the Path of the Warrior: years, sometimes decades, of drilling one narrow art of war until body and reflex both match its demands — matching framework.md's own note that this is exactly what closes the gap to a Space Marine's DX.\n\nDesign: 75 (Attribute Modifiers) + 15 (Enhanced Dodge 1) = 90 points, added to the Aeldari Physiology + Keen Battle-Sense baseline (154 + 5 = 159) for 249 points of physiology before skills. See the Aspect Warrior template for the full character, and Exarch's Mastery below for the further step up to Exarch.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Aeldari"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tPOxhgvykgNJ9t9fW",
					"name": "Attribute Modifiers (Aspect Training)",
					"local_notes": "Design: Framework target over the Aeldari Physiology + Keen Battle-Sense baseline: ST 11, DX 16, IQ 12 (unchanged), HT 11, Will 13, Per 13 (unchanged, already delivered by Keen Battle-Sense at this IQ). Increased ST +1 (B14, 10/level, 10) for ST 11 — enough muscle to wear Aspect armour and carry a fusion gun or exarch blade, nowhere near an Astartes' bulk. Increased DX +2 (B15, 20/level, 40) for DX 16, matching the anchor table's own note that this is exactly where centuries on one Path bring an Aspect Warrior's precision level with a Space Marine's own DX. Increased HT +1 (B15, 10/level, 10) for HT 11. Increased Will +1 (B16, 5/level, 5) for Will 13, the discipline of the shrine holding Emotional Intensity firmly in check. Basic Move +2 (B17, 5/level, 10) at the user's direction, so an Aspect Warrior (Move 12) matches a Battle-Brother. 10+40+10+5+10 = 75 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Physical"
					],
					"base_points": 75,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "st",
							"amount": 1
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
